<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. Nightingale returns for an adult preventive visit, booked separately from her ME/CFS care and scheduled at midday after a rest morning, with the visit done mostly seated or reclined. Her July cold cleared within two weeks without antibiotics, and the flare it caused settled after about ten days. At her ME/CFS review four weeks ago her fatigue and physical function scores had improved and her vitamin D level was replete. She has had no crashes since, works four to five hours a day from home in planned blocks, and goes out twice a week. Her last cervical screening was about four years ago and was normal. She has never had HIV, hepatitis B, or hepatitis C screening on record; at the March consultation the infectious diseases physician suggested completing these one-time tests with her preventive care. She completed the health risk assessment at home before the visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Visit</td><td>Adult preventive visit, established patient</td></tr><tr><td>Recent illness</td><td>Cold in July, resolved; flare settled in about ten days</td></tr><tr><td>Safety events</td><td>No falls, emergency visits, or hospital stays since starting care here</td></tr><tr><td>Medicines</td><td>Cholecalciferol daily; acetaminophen a few times a month</td></tr><tr><td>Active conditions</td><td>Post-infectious ME/CFS, moderate and improving; vitamin D deficiency, now replete</td></tr><tr><td>Functional status</td><td>Independent in self-care; works four to five hours a day from home; out of the house twice a week</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Health risk assessment. Completed by the patient before the visit and reviewed during the visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Finding</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Self-rated health</td><td>Fair, and better than in the spring</td></tr><tr><td>Psychosocial</td><td>Lives alone; colleagues visit to work with her and she keeps up a large correspondence; frustrated at times by her limits but not lonely or depressed</td></tr><tr><td>Behavioral</td><td>Never smoker; alcohol once or twice a month; no other drug use; simple diet with extra salt and 2 to 2.5 L of fluid a day; sleeps about eight hours with one waking</td></tr><tr><td>Activities of daily living</td><td>Independent in bathing, dressing, eating, toileting, and transfers, using a shower stool</td></tr><tr><td>Instrumental activities of daily living</td><td>Manages her own medicines, money, and work; groceries delivered and a housekeeper twice a week</td></tr><tr><td>Home safety</td><td>Smoke detectors working; ground-floor apartment; shower stool and a grab rail in the bathroom</td></tr><tr><td>Personal safety</td><td>Asked privately; she feels safe at home</td></tr><tr><td>Reproductive health</td><td>Not planning a pregnancy; uses a copper intrauterine device placed about two years ago</td></tr><tr><td>Vision, hearing, and dental</td><td>Reading glasses; no hearing concerns; dental check six months ago</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

